<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Building in Public - A 12 Week Backend Journal on Stephanie Tess van Slyck</title><link>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/</link><description>Recent content in Building in Public - A 12 Week Backend Journal on Stephanie Tess van Slyck</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><copyright>© 2026 Stephanie Tess van Slyck</copyright><lastBuildDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0200</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Where MCP Would Fit in a Project Like Standflow</title><link>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-10-02-where-mcp-would-fit/</link><pubDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-10-02-where-mcp-would-fit/</guid><description>Model Context Protocol is not a replacement for a REST API. A short reflection on what it is, and where it would actually belong in a system I have already built.</description></item><item><title>The Excursion I Missed, and Building for a Place I Had Not Seen</title><link>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-29-excursion-i-missed/</link><pubDate>Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-29-excursion-i-missed/</guid><description>The class visited the customer site. I could not attend, so this is about building for a context I only knew second-hand, and where the project stands.</description></item><item><title>Secrets, Escaping and Safe Choices</title><link>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-21-secrets-escaping-and-safe-choices/</link><pubDate>Mon, 21 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-21-secrets-escaping-and-safe-choices/</guid><description>How keys, escaping and consent are handled across my two projects, and why the safest choices were the ones that removed the unsafe option rather than guarding it.</description></item><item><title>Building a Whole Project with a Code Agent</title><link>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-18-building-a-whole-project-with-a-code-agent/</link><pubDate>Fri, 18 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-18-building-a-whole-project-with-a-code-agent/</guid><description>I used an AI code agent to take over and finish an entire group project, Standflow. This is the honest version: what the agent did, what I kept for myself, the guardrails that made it safe, the near-disaster, and what actually makes agentic development work.</description></item><item><title>Standflow: From a Manor-House Visit to a Working System</title><link>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-14-standflow-manor-house-to-system/</link><pubDate>Mon, 14 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-14-standflow-manor-house-to-system/</guid><description>The project case: a real customer with a real problem, the idea we proposed, and the system we sketched and then built to solve it.</description></item><item><title>Architecture of an LLM-Backed Rubric Assessor</title><link>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-04-rubric-assessor-architecture/</link><pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 09:30:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-09-04-rubric-assessor-architecture/</guid><description>How a small Express service turns a rubric and a student report into structured, schema-validated AI feedback: the data flow, the prompt contract, the error handling, and how it was tested without a real API call.</description></item><item><title>Building an AI Rubric Assessor: What Today Actually Taught Me</title><link>https://tessellation93.github.io/posts/2026-08-31-rubric-assessor-reflection/</link><pubDate>Mon, 31 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0200</pubDate><guid